        <Options>
            <Main applyHouseholdOperatingConditions="false" applyReducedOperatingConditions="false" atypicalElectricalLoads="false" waterConservation="false" referenceHouse="false">
                <Vermiculite code="1">
                    <English>Unknown</English>
                    <French>Inconnu</French>
                </Vermiculite>
            </Main>
            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>



&lt;b&gt;Observed Air Leakage:&lt;/b&gt;

Overall, your house is relatively air tight, however there are some key areas where air leakage occurs. Air leakage was observed through electrical outlets on your exterior walls, and around the weather stripping of windows and doors. 

The door sweep along the front door should be replaced or readjusted. 



Air leakage was also observed through all three bathroom exhaust fans. I recommend you replace your bathroom exhaust fans with an ENERGY STAR® rated model.



Air leakage is the biggest heat loss within your home, and I suspect the crawlspace/basement is largely to blame. 

Please see my "Special Note" comments under the Foundation Section of the report. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

For a minimum 10% improvement you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 300&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;13.2 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 500 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must have a home energy evaluation, which includes an air leakage test, before and after your retrofits. For a full rebate, the rebate application, with a copy of the first page of both home energy evaluation reports, must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of the second energy evaluation. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------





.</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The exterior walls of your house appear to be 2x6 construction with R20 batt insulation, as well as 1.5 inches of rigid insulation (assumed R7.5). In conjunction with a future upgrade to the exterior siding of this house, I have modelled adding another R7.5 of semi-rigid, breathable exterior insulation to the exterior of all walls.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By adding another R7.5 of semi-rigid, breathable exterior insulation, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 180&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;8.2 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 1,875 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 0.7 (R-3.8). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------</mainWalls>
                <foundation>

&lt;b&gt;Special Note: Crawlspace/Basement&lt;/b&gt;

Your crawlspace is technically a basement. It has a concrete wall, is taller than 4 feet, it can be accessed from inside the house, and the floor above is not insulated. It is heated by the boiler and there is no air or vapour barrier between the basement and the rest of the house. 



Moisture from the ground is a concern for basements with dirt floors. We know that warm air rises, and that humidity levels rise with the availability of moisture. In basements with no ground cover, the warm air rises, sucking up moisture from the dirt floor and could carry mould spores into the main-floor living area, through paths in any unsealed sections between the basement and main floor. This can seriously affect the health of the occupants. 



During my assesment, there did not appear to be any moisture issues within the basement. Ensure any exterior venting within the basement is open during the summer to vent the moisture, and closed during the winter to reduce heat loss. 



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------</foundation>
                <windows>

If you are considering replacing your 9 double paned windows, I have modeled the potential energy savings associated with upgrading all of them with ENERGY STAR® qualified windows rated for climate zone 3. In addition, I recommend triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt; 

By replacing those 9 windows with triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged, ENERGY STAR® qualified windows rated for climate zone 3, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 220&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, and decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;8.2 GJ&lt;/b&gt;. 



There are currently no rebates available for installing new windows from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

 

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------</windows>
                <HotWaterSystem>

Currently, your Oil-fired boiler is used to heat your hot water during the winter months and electricity is used

to heat your hot water during the summer months. (Winter is assumed to be 9 months of the year).



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By using your Oil-fired boiler year-round to heat your hot water, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 240&lt;/b&gt; per year in

energy savings.</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

&lt;b&gt;Boiler&lt;/b&gt;

If you are considering replacing your current Oil-fired boiler, I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STAR® qualified high-efficiency propane boiler with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE. This type of boiler is direct vented outside. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing your heating system with a high-efficiency condensing propane boiler with an efficiency of 95% AFUE, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 0&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;36.9 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 750 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

&lt;b&gt;Note:&lt;/b&gt; Due to recent oil prices, savings are minimal when switching to propane, however historically propane has been a cheaper source of fuel. 





&lt;b&gt;Pellet Stove&lt;/b&gt;

If you are considering installing pellet stove, I recommend you install a pellet stove that is EPA Certified. Ensure that the installation complies with local building and fire codes, and includes a dedicated combustion air supply. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By installing an EPA Certified pellet stove and using it for 50% of your heating load, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 1,100&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 750 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.





&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------</HeatingSystem>
                <AdditionalComments>

&lt;b&gt;Domestic Hot Water:&lt;/b&gt;

Currently, your Oil-fired boiler is used to heat your hot water during the winter months and electricity is used

to heat your hot water during the summer months. (Winter is assumed to be 9 months of the year).



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By using your Oil-fired boiler year-round to heat your hot water, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 240&lt;/b&gt; per year in

energy costs.





&lt;b&gt;Heat Recovery Ventilator:&lt;/b&gt;

An HRV saves on energy costs compared to conventional ventilation systems because it recovers heat from exhausted air. The HRV draws in stale, indoor air and passes it through a heat exchanger. The exchanger captures most of the heat before it exhausts the stale air outside. At the same time, the HRV draws outdoor air in for filtering, passes it through the other half of the heat exchanger where it collects heat from the exhausted air, and finally distributes the outdoor air throughout the house. The warmed, outdoor air is distributed through an existing forced-air distribution system or a dedicated ductwork system.



I recommend you clean your HRV exchanger every 3 months and the machine at least once a year. 

Allowing your HRV to work efficiently will help keep bills low, but having the filters clean will allow it to operate with more effectiveness. The purpose of an HRV is to increase the indoor air quality of your home, and when the system isnt cleaned, dirt is transferred into the air that is entering your home. Over time, your HRV filters can accumulate a lot of dirt and debris from all the air passing through them. The best way to clean them is by sliding them out from their tracks in the unit and washing them in warm, soapy water. Do not use a pressure washer to clean your filters as you will likely damage them and have to buy new ones, when a soapy wash would have done the trick! Make sure to let them dry thoroughly before you place them back into your unit.



&lt;b&gt;Fuel Costs - Yellowknife&lt;/b&gt;

The following values have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:

Electricity - $19.45 min, $0.30 per kWh

Oil  - $0.84 per litre 

Propane - $0.71 per litre

Wood - $341 per cord 

Pellets - $6.58 per bag



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

&lt;b&gt;Disclaimer&lt;/b&gt;

The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building.  This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.





&lt;b&gt;Your Energy Advisor&lt;/b&gt;



Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.



Sincerely,



&lt;b&gt;Michael Goodwin

Energy Management Specialist

Arctic Energy Alliance&lt;/b&gt;

Phone: (867) 920-3333

Fax: (867) 873-0303

E-mail: michael.goodwin@aea.nt.ca



